WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.453
What an engineering team should actually produce.

2
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.600
Compliance work goes faster, and is more honest, when engineering supplies concrete artifacts.

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.656
Five that serve almost every regime: An AI inventory.

4
00:00:12.936 --> 00:00:20.381
Every feature that calls a model: purpose, users, model and version, provider, data sent, outputs, who reviews them.

5
00:00:20.661 --> 00:00:23.413
This is the input to every risk classification.

6
00:00:23.693 --> 00:00:25.208
Disclosures and marking.

7
00:00:25.488 --> 00:00:35.963
User-facing notice that people are interacting with AI, and machine-readable marking of generated media where required, implemented and tested like any other feature.

8
00:00:36.243 --> 00:00:37.629
Logs and retention.

9
00:00:37.909 --> 00:00:44.139
What you log, for how long, who can see it, and how it is deleted (Studio 6 covers the privacy side).

10
00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:49.624
High-risk regimes require logs; data protection limits them; design for both.

11
00:00:49.904 --> 00:00:51.867
Documentation from and for others.

12
00:00:52.147 --> 00:01:05.203
The model provider’s documentation and usage policies, filed with your system’s own documentation: intended use, known limitations, eval results, and the model selection record from lesson 3.

13
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:06.784
An incident process.

14
00:01:07.064 --> 00:01:14.531
How a harmful output or misuse is detected, triaged, fixed, and, where the law requires, reported, with named owners.

15
00:01:14.811 --> 00:01:22.085
The labs now report incidents to regulators within days; your process should at least reach your own leadership within hours.

16
00:01:22.365 --> 00:01:34.312
The NIST AI Risk Management Framework is a useful voluntary structure for organizing these (govern, map, measure, manage), and procurement teams increasingly ask for it by name.
